<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/advanced/population-and-migration-geography">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Population geography maps <strong>who lives where, how fast that changes, and who moves —
    and why</strong>. Two reversals define the present moment. First, after a century of
    assuming endless growth, the numbers have turned: the world's population is still rising —
    about 8.2 billion in 2024 — but the UN's 2024 revision now projects a <em>peak</em> around
    10.3 billion in the mid-2080s, followed by slow decline, because fertility has fallen
    faster than any forecaster dared write down. Second, the map of age has split in two:
    Japan, Italy and South Korea are shrinking and ageing while Africa's median age remains
    about 19. Migration — always contested, always constant — is the hinge connecting the
    halves: young regions, ageing regions, and the flows between them. Getting this chapter
    right means reading three instruments fluently: growth rates, population structures, and
    the push–pull machinery of movement.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the transition, the reversal, and the flows between them" widget="equationStep" config={popCfg} />

  <h2>Populations grow and decline</h2>
  <p>
    Growth is a race between two counters: births and deaths. <strong>Natural change</strong>
    is the birth rate minus the death rate, usually expressed per 1,000 people per year
    (migration is added separately to get total change). The mechanics of the race are
    asymmetric, and that asymmetry explains history: death rates can fall in a decade — clean
    water, vaccination, antibiotics — while birth rates take generations to follow, because
    they are bound up with women's education, urbanisation, child survival and income. That
    gap <em>is</em> the population explosion. Worked through with the
    <strong>rule of 70</strong>: a country growing at 1% a year doubles in about 70 ÷ 1 = 70
    years; at 3.75% a year — roughly Niger's rate — in about 19. Now watch the numbers turn:
    the world's growth <em>rate</em> crested above 2% a year in the early 1960s and has since
    fallen under 1%; the annual <em>increase</em> peaked at around 90 million people in the
    late 1980s and has eased to roughly 70 million. In April 2023 India passed China as the
    most populous country — not because India grew faster that year, but because China's
    fertility collapsed below replacement decades ago. Over half of all countries are now
    below replacement fertility (~2.1 children per woman), and South Korea records the
    world's lowest at around 0.7 — a level at which each generation is a third the size of
    the one before it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the world's population is growing faster than ever." The
    absolute number of people has never been higher, but the <em>growth rate</em> peaked in
    the 1960s and has more than halved since. Confusing a large level with a fast rate is the
    single most common error in population discussions — and it inverts the real policy
    problem, which is now as much about shrinking and ageing as about boom.
  </p>
  <p>
    The arithmetic is worth doing by hand at least once. Take a country with a crude birth
    rate of 28 and a crude death rate of 8 per 1,000: natural increase is 20 per 1,000 —
    2.0% a year — and the rule of 70 converts that to a doubling in about 35 years, before
    any migration is counted. A country with birth rate 9 and death rate 11 is already
    shrinking by 0.2% a year: a loss of roughly a fifth of its people in a century. Neither
    number requires a single policy change to matter more than a generation of infrastructure
    planning. And because rates move for social reasons, the levers are social too: birth
    rates fall reliably with girls' education, child survival, urbanisation and access to
    contraception — the "empowerment route" the transition model quietly encodes. Governments
    try to push the counters directly, and the record is instructive: China's anti-natalist
    one-child policy (1980) bent the curve at enormous human cost and left the age structure
    damaged, and its successor pro-natalist three-child policy (2021) has so far moved
    fertility barely at all; Romania's 1966 Decree 770 banned abortion and contraception and
    produced a birth spike, a surge of unsafe abortions and a cohort of institutionalised
    children — one of the clearest natural experiments showing that decrees cannot outmuscle
    the social forces behind fertility. France's long family policy (childcare, tax
    allowances, parental leave) is the sustained-investment version; its honest result is
    fertility among Europe's highest at around 1.6 — better than its neighbours, still far
    from replacement.
  </p>

  <h2>Population structures change over time</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>population pyramid</strong> is the age–sex structure drawn as back-to-back bar
    charts, and it is a photograph of a country's past with its future attached. A wide base
    (Niger, DR Congo) means high birth rates and decades of built-in growth; a barrel shape
    (Brazil, Mexico) means the transition is mid-walk; an inverted top (Japan, Italy) means
    decline is already scheduled. The shape changes through the <strong>demographic
    transition model</strong>: stage 1, high birth and death rates, near-stable numbers (no
    whole country remains, though a few isolated populations fit); stage 2, death rates fall,
    birth rates stay high — the fastest growth (Niger, DR Congo); stage 3, birth rates fall as
    urbanisation and education spread (India, Bangladesh, Mexico); stage 4, both low, near
    stability (the UK, China); stage 5, deaths exceed births (Japan, South Korea, Italy). The
    pyramid's summary statistic is the <strong>dependency ratio</strong>: the under-15s plus
    over-65s per 100 people of working age. Worked example: a country with 30% under 15 and
    5% over 65 carries (30 + 5) ÷ 65 × 100 ≈ <strong>54 dependants per 100 workers</strong>;
    Japan today, with roughly 11% under 15 and 29% over 65, carries about
    <strong>69 per 100</strong> — and its working-age support ratio has fallen from about 12
    workers per pensioner in 1950 to about 2. One more mechanism matters: <strong>population
    momentum</strong>. Even if a youthful country's fertility dropped to replacement tomorrow,
    its population would keep growing for decades, because the huge cohorts already born are
    still moving into their childbearing years. Momentum is why the mid-2080s peak is already
    largely written into today's age structure.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "ageing is only a rich-country problem." The demographic
    transition is compressing: France needed over a century for its over-65 share to double
    from 7% to 14%; China, Brazil and Thailand are each managing the same crossing in about
    two to three decades — while still middle-income, before pension systems and care
    infrastructure are built. Most of the world's over-65s will live in developing countries
    within a generation, which is why "get old before getting rich" is the phrase demographers
    actually worry about.
  </p>

  <h2>The causes and impacts of international migration</h2>
  <p>
    About 281 million people — 3.6% of humanity — live outside their country of birth, and the
    theory of why was laid out by Ravenstein's "laws" in 1885 and sharpened by Everett Lee in
    1966: migration is the outcome of <strong>push factors</strong> at origin (unemployment,
    conflict, drought, land scarcity), <strong>pull factors</strong> at destination (wages,
    safety, education, family already there), <strong>intervening obstacles</strong> (visas,
    deserts, seas, the price of a smuggler) and <strong>personal factors</strong> (who is free
    and able to move — the young, the skilled, the connected). Worked into reality: the
    Mexico–US corridor of around 11 million people is overwhelmingly an economic
    wage-gradient flow; the nearly 8 million Venezuelans who left after 2015 are a
    collapse-of-order flow; the more than 120 million people forcibly displaced worldwide
    (UNHCR, 2024) are conflict made visible. Two structural facts discipline the whole topic.
    First, <strong>internal migration dwarfs international migration</strong>: China's
    <em>floating population</em> of rural workers inside China is around 370 million — more
    than all the world's international migrants combined. Second, the money flows
    <strong>home</strong>: remittances to low- and middle-income countries run at roughly
    $650–670 billion a year — about three times all foreign aid combined — and in the
    Philippines they amount to about 8.5% of GDP. Impacts cut both ways: destination
    economies fill labour gaps and age balances (a quarter to a third of Britain's doctors
    trained abroad); origin economies gain the remittances and lose the people — the
    "brain drain" that modern evidence partly reframes as "brain circulation", since skills,
    capital and returnees flow back as corridors mature.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two definitions carry legal weight and should be used precisely. A <strong>refugee</strong>
    (1951 Refugee Convention) is a person outside their country with a well-founded fear of
    persecution — a status, not a synonym for "migrant" — while an <strong>economic
    migrant</strong> moves primarily for work and living standards; the mixed flows of real
    crises (the Syrian, Venezuelan and Ukrainian displacements contained both) are exactly why
    asylum systems are contested. And the remittance arithmetic deserves a worked example: a
    single construction worker in Dubai sending home $200 a month moves $2,400 a year. At a
    million workers — smaller than several real corridors — that is $2.4 billion a year,
    rivalling the entire capital budget of a ministry, delivered without conditions, advisors
    or overhead. Multiplied across the global system it becomes the $650-billion-plus flow
    that outweighs aid, and it explains why finance ministries from Manila to Kathmandu watch
    Gulf and East Asian labour markets as closely as their own.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "most migrants move from poor countries to rich ones." Most
    international migration is <strong>South–South</strong> — between developing countries
    (a Kenyan worker in Tanzania, a Burkinaabé farmer in Côte d'Ivoire) — and internal
    migration dwarfs everything. Headlines show the wealthy-world border; the data show the
    neighbouring country.
  </p>

  <h2>Counting people: the data underneath</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure above rests on two instruments. The <strong>census</strong> aims to count
    everyone once, at one moment; the <strong>vital registration</strong> system records each
    birth and death as it happens. Rich countries run both; many of the countries with the
    fastest growth run neither well — a large share of births and deaths in low-income
    countries are never registered, which is why demographers lean on sample surveys and
    modelled estimates, and why the UN revises its numbers every few years. The practical
    consequences run through this whole chapter: the countries whose age structures matter
    most for 2060 are the ones whose data is least certain, and migration — the hardest
    variable of all, because it is fast, illegal-when-irregular, and invisible to registries —
    is the largest error bar in every population projection. Scepticism about demographic
    statistics is not cynicism; it is the discipline the subject runs on.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Malthus and his rematches.</strong> Malthus (1798) argued population grows
    geometrically while food grows arithmetically, so misery must cap it; the Green
    Revolution's fertiliser-and-dwarf-wheat package deferred his forecast by half a century,
    and Ehrlich's <em>Population Bomb</em> (1968) missed the fertility collapse that was
    already under way. Boserup's counter-theory — population pressure <em>drives</em>
    agricultural intensification — captures the other half of the truth. The live rematch
    concerns climate and water rather than calories: the World Bank's Groundswell modelling
    projects up to 216 million <em>internal</em> climate migrants by 2050, mostly rural-to-urban
    within countries — not the international flows headlines fear. <strong>The dividend
    arithmetic.</strong> When fertility falls while the large young cohorts are still
    children, the dependency ratio drops to its minimum and, if jobs exist, growth
    accelerates — East Asia's "demographic dividend", worth a substantial share of its
    1965–1990 growth by World Bank estimates; Africa's version is the development question of
    the century, and it is conditional on employment, education and governance, not automatic.
    <strong>The low-fertility puzzle.</strong> Standard theory said fertility would settle near
    replacement; instead it keeps falling across rich and middle-income countries alike
    (China's total fertility is around 1.0–1.2, South Korea's about 0.7), and explanations —
    housing costs, precarious work, gender-unequal care burdens — are still contested.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> whether African fertility follows the fast East Asian
    path or a slower one (the single biggest swing factor in every world projection), whether
    pro-natalist policies move fertility more than marginally (the honest record: barely), and
    how ageing societies should set immigration, retirement age and automation policy when the
    pension arithmetic fails quietly rather than loudly.
  </p>

